 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to certificates of residence\nrequired of community college enrollees",
    "basePrintNo": "S8994",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8994-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8994\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to certificates of resi-\ndence required of community college enrollees\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow community college students to obtain a certificate of residence\nfrom their county of residence electronically on a permanent basis.\nAdditionally, this bill mandates the removal of social security numbers\nfrom all county certificate of residence applications in order to make\nresident tuition accessible to all eligible students.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 allows community college students to apply for certificates of\nresidence from counties via electronic means on and after July 1, 2023,\nand prohibits counties from mandating social security numbers as a\ncondition of application for a certificate of residence. It also author-\nizes the SUNY Board of Trustees to promulgate regulations to clarify the\nprocess for students to appeal decisions by county fiscal officers to\nreject applications for certificates of residence. If a student can\ndemonstrate hardship in meeting the deadline to acquire the certificate\nof residence, the SUNY Chancellor may overturn the county's decision to\nreject the student's application for a certificate of residence.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn order to be eligible for resident tuition at community colleges,\nstudents must obtain a certificate of residence from the county of their\npermanent residence and submit the certificate to the College within the\nfirst 30 days of each semester. Often counties require students to\nobtain certificates of residence in-person. This can be prohibitive to\nstudents who cannot travel to the County Office during regular business\nhours. Under EO 202.11 during the COVID-19 pandemic, students were\nprovided the opportunity to obtain such certificates via electronic\nmeans. This legislation would allow students to continue to be able to\napply for certificates of residence either in person or online.\n\nThere is no requirement that students provide social security numbers to\nprove residency, yet some counties require that students, or parents of\nstudents under 21, submit a social security number within order to\nobtain a certificate of residence. This can be prohibitive to students\nwho may not have a social security number, while meeting all require-\nments necessary to demonstrate permanent residence.\n\nIf a student is not approved by their home county to receive a certif-\nicate of residence, they often end up owing thousands of dollars more\nthan they were expecting to pay for their classes at the community\ncollege. By the time a student realizes the error, it may be too late to\napply for student loans, leaving that amount as an immediate concern for\nthe student and potentially derailing their academic career. The\ndocumentation process can be challenging or confusing to students and\nmust be done in a compressed time frame, and denial of a certificate of\nresidence is an extreme penalty to place on students who may have been\n\nlifelong residents of New York State and deserve to receive the lower\nresident rate of tuition.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
